# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. github.com/Danfr4nk/wikibrain
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Some rules below name paths that do not exist in this repository yet —
# corpus/, shelf/, intake/, raw/facebook-threads/. They are kept anyway. A
# rule that guards an absent path costs nothing and is already in force on
# the day that path arrives; adding it afterwards is how data gets published
# by accident. Do not prune them for tidiness.
#
# This file is the UNION of two rule sets, merged per the procedure in
# MIGRATION.md: the original wiki-brain-main-1 rules (above) and the corpus
# rules (below). Neither side was dropped. Merging them is the whole point —
# the top half is Dan's decision about Dan, the bottom half is a decision
# about ~498 other people, and they are not the same decision.

# THE INTAKE LEDGER'S DATA IS TRACKED, and this repository is public.
#
# That is a decision, not an oversight, and it was made twice: the guard was
# restored on 2026-08-30 when the "repo is private now" premise turned out to be
# false, and removed again the same day when the operator asked for the ledger to
# sync while the repository is public, stating they understood the implications.
#
# What that means concretely: `intake/events.jsonl` is a public, permanent record
# of consumption, readable by anyone, and git history cannot be un-published. It
# is tracked because the wiki has to be able to read it — a sealed or ignored
# ledger is invisible to the analysis it exists to feed.
#
# To reverse: make the repository private FIRST, verify it, and only then decide
# whether the ignore lines are still wanted. In that order.

# --- corpus rules ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# The message corpus is not Dan's data alone. It contains the phone numbers,
# email addresses and private words of ~498 other people, none of whom chose
# to be published. The default here is closed.
#
# git history cannot be un-published. Un-ignoring any line below puts that
# data on the public internet permanently, for everyone in it.

# Raw corpus — every message, in the clear.
corpus/messages.csv

# Identity material and the hashing salt. The salt is what stops a published
# thread_id from being brute-forced back to a phone number: there are only
# ~10^10 US numbers, so an unsalted hash is not anonymisation, it is a speed
# bump. If this leaks, every derived artifact de-anonymises with it.
corpus/private/

# Shelved fragment exports — superseded, retained locally as error evidence.
# See CORPUS_POLICY.md. Same personal data as the corpus, same reasoning.
#
# Note what these lines do NOT do: the source Google Sheet is deliberately
# shared "anyone with the link" (see corpus/README.md), so the corpus is
# already reachable by URL. Ignoring it here keeps it out of git history,
# which is permanent and searchable. That is worth doing on its own terms —
# but it is not what is protecting this data, and it should not be mistaken
# for that.
shelf/**
!shelf/README.md
!shelf/MANIFEST.md

# Facebook thread bodies — the same decision as corpus/messages.csv, for the
# same reason. These are ~500 other people's private words, they were never
# published, and git history is permanent. The MANIFEST.json beside them IS
# tracked: thread-level counts, date ranges and counterparty names are the
# existence-evidence the operator asked for, and they carry no message bodies.
#
# Not an ingestion filter. The bodies sit on disk, bin/wb-corroborate reads them
# in full, and every inference that should rest on them can. Re-fetchable from
# the public Drive tree at any time — see raw/SOURCES.md.
raw/facebook-threads/*.txt
